          "text": "{\"category\":\"trigger_mask_or_channel_configuration_mismatch\",\"cause\":\"Unknown; the leading hypothesis is that channels 32/33 are intentionally masked or inconsistently configured between acquisition and AutoDQM, causing persistent low occupancy, abnormal pulse statistics, and missing-channel flags.\",\"action\":\"First verify the channel-32/33 digitizer enable, zero-suppression, trigger-mask, and AutoDQM expected-channel configurations for all subruns, especially around subrun 211. Compare raw digitizer presence and rates with trigger-board data. If the channels are intentionally masked, update AutoDQM expectations; if they should be active, inspect/reseat the shared channel-pair signal path and test in a short run while monitoring channel presence and board matching. Do not unmask them until noise and trigger-rate behavior are checked.\",\"confidence\":0.68,\"supporting_historical_runs\":[1702,1703,2068,2126],\"reasoning_summary\":\"OBSERVED: 740/741 subruns are anomalous, channel 32 has extreme deviations in occupancy, pulse count, baseline, and pulse-height features, and channels 32/33 repeatedly appear missing. Both are listed among masked physical pins. LVDS telemetry has no persistently missing, zero, low, or high pin, including the pin mapped to channels 32/33. INFERRED: a persistent mask/configuration mismatch is more plausible than a demonstrated LVDS failure; runs 1702/1703 provide only partial analogy for configuration-driven feature shifts, while 2068/2126 partially resemble missing-channel cases. CONTRADICTING/UNKNOWN: masking alone may not explain waveform-feature shifts, and digitizer-rate consistency, board matching, queue state, synchronization, and within-run configuration changes are unavailable. There is no exact historical match.\"}",
